<PageTitle>Identities · louis-agent</PageTitle>
<PageHead Description="Who an agent is, as files: its voice, its roles and the principles every agent reads first, and the identities that run today." />

<Band Id="top" Kicker="Identities">
    <h2 class="chrome">Who an agent is, as files.</h2>
    <p class="lede">An identity is who the agent is: its voice, its modes, its roles and the packs it names, all markdown in a project of its own. The engine runs any identity you hand it. Your program picks one, or several, the way this site's program runs the coding agent and the guide in its corner.</p>
    <GraphView Spec="IdentityGraph.Spec" Title="An identity, what it holds, and the level each part speaks at" />
</Band>

<Band Id="parts" Kicker="What an identity holds">
    <ul class="rules">
        <li><strong>The principles, first.</strong> Every prompt the engine builds opens with the same principles file, built into the engine, before the voice or any role, so nothing later in the prompt can push it aside. <a href="/identities#the-principles">Read them</a>.</li>
        <li><strong>A voice.</strong> Who it is and how it speaks: one file per product, and a product may have more than one mode (Puls speaks Afrikaans or English, and the coding agent has a voice for fixing a service's errors). A session that names no role gets the voice.</li>
        <li><strong>Roles.</strong> A role is a second session with only the tools its task needs and a file that says how to work and what to report back. Puls's specialists are roles: a researcher, a reader, a historian, an analyst and a cook.</li>
        <li><strong>Minions.</strong> A small job on a local model, one file each, so a specialist hands off the cheap part of its work.</li>
        <li><strong>The packs it names.</strong> One class says which packs the identity uses, and their toolsets are what its sessions may load. An identity knows its packs by name and never references one.</li>
        <li><strong>By level.</strong> A session's depth is its level, and it loads a personality only from its level's folder: the voice at level 0, the roles at level 1, the minions at level 2. A layout test fails a file outside the known folders or a name in two of them.</li>
    </ul>
</Band>

<Band Id="the-principles" Kicker="The principles, as every agent reads them">
    <p class="lede">This is the engine's own file, shown as it is. It opens every prompt, for every agent on the framework, and a test holds this page to the file.</p>
    <PrinciplesView />
</Band>

<Band Id="levels" Kicker="The four that exist">
    <CardDeck>
        <Card Title="Puls" Summary="The South African assistant: two voices, five specialists, two minions." Tag="louis-agent.identity.puls">
            <p>A voice in Afrikaans and one in English, five specialists as roles, two minions, and the packs that find a shop, estimate prices and read recipes.</p>
        </Card>
        <Card Title="The coding agent" Summary="Works on your repository with the files, git, dotnet, shell and web packs." Tag="louis-agent.identity.coding">
            <p>Its tools are the files, git, dotnet, shell and web packs, and it has a second voice for the orchestrator that has it fix a service's errors.</p>
        </Card>
        <Card Title="The guide" Summary="The expert in this site's corner: one voice on one pack, the framework's own docs." Tag="louis-agent.identity.expert">
            <p>One voice on one pack, the framework's own docs, read-only. It answers about the framework from the docs, plainly to a visitor and in full to a developer.</p>
        </Card>
        <Card Title="The triage team" Summary="A manager and three roles that triage a service's errors: a sorter, an investigator, a reviewer." Tag="louis-agent.identity.triage">
            <p>A manager as the voice, the hub every result passes through, and three roles: a sorter that names the department, an investigator that finds the cause and its area, and a reviewer that judges a fix. The coder is the coding agent. The workflow that runs them on a service's errors is being built.</p>
        </Card>
    </CardDeck>
</Band>

<Band Id="yours" Kicker="Yours">
    <div class="panel">
        <h3>A folder of markdown and one class.</h3>
        <p>The guide in the corner is the smallest identity there is: a class that names the docs pack, and a voice file that says who it is, how it answers and where the docs are. Your own is the same shape: write the voice, add a role for each kind of work, name the packs, and your program hands it to a host. <a href="/build-your-own">How a program does that</a>.</p>
    </div>
</Band>
